 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: keys are always contiguous (`t1,t2,…`). `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order and resets `next` to
+  `tabs.length + 1`, so URLs never show gaps (`?tabs=t1,t2`, not `t1,t4,t7`).
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys (user overrode it explicitly, 2026-10-23, for tidy URLs).
+- Known cost (accepted by the maintainer): keys to the right of a closed tab shift, so those tabs remount and lose scroll
+  position and unsaved form input (the 2026-08-29 incident).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers remaining tabs contiguously")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — SUPERSEDED by §contiguous-tab-keys (2026-10-23)
 - Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+- Decision (superseded): a key was assigned once from `next` and never reused or shifted; closing a tab left a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Reason (historical): the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the
+  closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29).
